[SECTION]
TITLE: Context

[TEXT]
Roadmap feature: **UTF8-BOUNDARY-API** (tier 1, status: next).

This feature adds two formal subprograms to the `Anystrings` generic
signature (`src/anystrings.ads`) plus a shared, non-generic Result type
and error enumeration:

- `To_UTF_8 (Source : String_Type) return String`
- `From_UTF_8 (Source : String) return UTF_8_Decode_Result`

The goal is to establish a canonical UTF-8 interchange boundary so that
generic clients (e.g. `aclida_style`) can round-trip any `String_Type`
value through UTF-8 without backend-specific knowledge.

All four bundled backends (`standard`, `wide`, `wide_wide`, `uxstring`)
MUST provide concrete implementations of these formals.

[TEXT]
Design decisions captured during Phase 1 review:

1. **Result-pattern over exceptions**: `From_UTF_8` SHALL NOT raise.  It
   returns a discriminated record (`UTF_8_Decode_Result`) carrying either
   a converted `String_Type` value or one of a finite set of named error
   codes.  This keeps the API SPARK-friendly and removes the need for a
   separate `Try_From_UTF_8` variant.
2. **Surrogate handling in `To_UTF_8`**: any code point in the surrogate
   range U+D800-U+DFFF that a backend can legally hold SHALL be encoded
   as U+FFFD (REPLACEMENT CHARACTER) by `To_UTF_8`.  `To_UTF_8` is total
   and never raises.
3. **Round-trip identity**: `From_UTF_8 (To_UTF_8 (S))` SHALL yield a
   success Result whose value equals `S` for every `String_Type` value
   `S` representable by the backend AND containing no surrogate code
   units.  Strings containing surrogates lose them on round-trip
   (substituted by U+FFFD); this case is documented but does not violate
   the invariant for the supported domain.
4. **Inverse round-trip**: `To_UTF_8 (value-of (From_UTF_8 (B)))` is NOT
   required to equal `B` byte-for-byte; backends MAY normalise (e.g.
   NFC).  Any normalisation MUST be documented in the backend header.

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Distinction from the Latin-1 boundary

[TEXT]
The existing `To_Latin_1` / `To_String_Type` pair converts between
`String_Type` and an Ada `String` whose code positions are ISO 8859-1
(Latin-1) values - a byte-for-byte identity in the `standard` backend.

`To_UTF_8` and `From_UTF_8` operate on an Ada `String` whose bytes
constitute a **UTF-8 encoded** byte sequence.  The two boundaries are
semantically distinct:

- A Latin-1 `String` may contain code positions 128-255 as single bytes;
  those same code points expand to two bytes in UTF-8.
- An all-ASCII `String` (positions 0-127) is identical in both encodings.
- Backends that operate natively on Unicode (e.g. `uxstring`) MUST encode
  their full Unicode range into UTF-8, not truncate to Latin-1.

Implementations MUST NOT conflate the two boundaries.

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Shared Result Type and Error Enumeration

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-001
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: Shared UTF-8 decode-error enumeration
STATEMENT: >>>
The library SHALL define a non-generic, public enumeration type
`UTF_8_Decode_Error` enumerating every distinguishable failure category
of UTF-8 decoding.  At minimum, the enumeration SHALL include:

- `Invalid_Lead_Byte`        - A byte that cannot start any UTF-8 sequence
  (e.g. 16#80#-16#BF#, 16#C0#-16#C1#, 16#F5#-16#FF#).
- `Truncated_Sequence`       - A multi-byte sequence is cut short by end
  of input.
- `Invalid_Continuation`     - A continuation byte does not satisfy the
  16#80#-16#BF# range constraint after a valid lead byte.
- `Overlong_Encoding`        - A code point is encoded with more bytes
  than required (e.g. U+002F as 16#C0# 16#AF#).
- `Surrogate_Code_Point`     - A decoded code point falls in the
  surrogate range U+D800-U+DFFF (forbidden by RFC 3629).
- `Code_Point_Out_Of_Range`  - A decoded code point is greater than
  U+10FFFF.

The enumeration is part of the public API and is referenced by the
result type (FUNC-UTF8-002) and by `From_UTF_8` (FUNC-UTF8-008).
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Distinct error variants give callers the option to log, recover, or
report precisely.  The enumeration is closed (no `Other`/`Unknown`
catch-all) to make exhaustive `case` analysis decidable in SPARK.

The enumeration MUST be declared in a non-generic package - backends
cannot import a type that is internal to the generic.  Suggested home:
a new child package such as `Anystrings.UTF_8` or a sibling
`Anystrings_UTF_8`.  The exact package placement is a tech-spec
decision (Phase 2); this requirement only mandates that the
enumeration be reachable from both `src/anystrings.ads` (the generic)
and every backend `string_types.ads`.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-002
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: Shared UTF-8 decode-result discriminated record
STATEMENT: >>>
The library SHALL define a non-generic, public discriminated record type
parameterised by the user's `String_Type` such that an instance carries
either a successfully decoded `String_Type` value or one of the
`UTF_8_Decode_Error` variants from FUNC-UTF8-001.

The shape of this type SHALL satisfy the following invariants regardless
of the chosen Ada syntax (generic instantiation, formal type, or
sub-package):

- Exactly one boolean-or-equivalent discriminant indicates success vs.
  failure.
- On success the type provides access to a `Value` of type `String_Type`.
- On failure the type provides access to a `Reason` of type
  `UTF_8_Decode_Error`.
- The two cases are mutually exclusive at the type level (no record can
  carry both).

Public, total accessor functions SHALL be provided:

- `Is_Ok (R) return Boolean`
- `Get_Value (R) return String_Type`  with `Pre => Is_Ok (R)`
- `Get_Error (R) return UTF_8_Decode_Error` with `Pre => not Is_Ok (R)`

The exact name of the record type is `UTF_8_Decode_Result`.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
The discriminated-record shape (rather than a tagged-type hierarchy) is
chosen for SPARK compatibility and because it sidesteps allocation: a
result is a stack value.

Because the type is parameterised by `String_Type`, it may need to be a
formal of a child generic, or its declaration may need to live inside a
helper generic that is instantiated alongside `Anystrings`.  Tech-spec
will pick one of these strategies; the requirement here is the
observable surface (Is_Ok, Get_Value, Get_Error).

The `Pre` contracts on `Get_Value` / `Get_Error` make the type
SPARK-provable: callers must check `Is_Ok` before extracting a value.
<<<

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Functional Requirements - To_UTF_8

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-003
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: To_UTF_8 formal subprogram in the generic signature
STATEMENT: >>>
The `Anystrings` generic signature SHALL include a formal function

    with function To_UTF_8 (Source : String_Type) return String;

that converts a `String_Type` value to an Ada `String` whose bytes
constitute a well-formed UTF-8 encoded byte sequence representing the
same text as `Source`.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
This is a total function: every `String_Type` value MUST have a defined
UTF-8 representation.  Backends that use a Unicode-capable internal
encoding (e.g. UXStrings) encode their full code-point range.  Backends
that store Latin-1 (the `standard` backend) treat each stored byte as its
Unicode code-point equivalent and encode it into UTF-8 accordingly
(code points 0-127 are unchanged; code points 128-255 expand to two UTF-8
bytes).  Wide and Wide_Wide backends encode their respective
`Wide_Character` / `Wide_Wide_Character` repertoires.

Implementations MUST NOT produce the same result as `To_Latin_1` for
strings containing code points above U+007F.

Source file: `src/anystrings.ads`
All four backend directories must provide a concrete binding.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-004
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: To_UTF_8 on an empty string
STATEMENT: >>>
When `Source` is the empty `String_Type` value, `To_UTF_8 (Source)` SHALL
return an empty `String` (i.e. `""`).
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Empty-input behaviour MUST be consistent across all backends.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-005
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: To_UTF_8 ASCII fast-path correctness
STATEMENT: >>>
For any `String_Type` value whose logical character sequence consists
exclusively of code points in the range U+0000-U+007F (ASCII), `To_UTF_8`
SHALL return a `String` equal to the byte-for-byte representation of those
code points (i.e. single-byte UTF-8 sequences).
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
This requirement asserts that backends do not apply unexpected encoding
overhead to pure-ASCII input and provides a simple cross-backend
consistency check.  It does not mandate a specific code path or branch
optimisation.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-006
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: To_UTF_8 output is always well-formed UTF-8
STATEMENT: >>>
The `String` returned by `To_UTF_8` SHALL always constitute a well-formed
UTF-8 byte sequence as defined by RFC 3629.  No backend implementation
SHALL produce output that is not valid UTF-8.

Specifically, the output SHALL NOT contain:

- The encoding of any code point in U+D800-U+DFFF (surrogate range).
- The encoding of any code point greater than U+10FFFF.
- Overlong encodings.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-007
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: To_UTF_8 substitutes surrogate code points with U+FFFD
STATEMENT: >>>
For backends whose internal character type can hold values in the
surrogate range U+D800-U+DFFF (notably the `wide` and `wide_wide`
backends), `To_UTF_8` SHALL encode each such code unit as the UTF-8
encoding of U+FFFD (REPLACEMENT CHARACTER, the three bytes
16#EF# 16#BF# 16#BD#).

`To_UTF_8` SHALL NOT raise for any reason, including the presence of
surrogate code units in the input.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
This makes `To_UTF_8` total under the project's "no exceptions in the
contract surface" rule.  Substitution with U+FFFD is the standard
recovery strategy described by the Unicode Standard (Sec 3.9 "Unicode
Encoding Forms").

Backends whose internal character type cannot represent surrogates
(e.g. `standard` with `Character` 0..255, `uxstring` which already
guarantees scalar values) MAY satisfy this requirement vacuously.
<<<

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Functional Requirements - From_UTF_8

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-008
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: From_UTF_8 formal subprogram in the generic signature
STATEMENT: >>>
The `Anystrings` generic signature SHALL include a formal function

    with function From_UTF_8 (Source : String) return UTF_8_Decode_Result;

that converts an Ada `String` whose bytes are intended to constitute a
UTF-8 encoded byte sequence into a `UTF_8_Decode_Result` value
(FUNC-UTF8-002).

On well-formed UTF-8 input, the returned value SHALL satisfy
`Is_Ok (Result) = True` and `Get_Value (Result)` SHALL be the
corresponding `String_Type` value.

On any input that is not a valid UTF-8 byte sequence per RFC 3629,
the returned value SHALL satisfy `Is_Ok (Result) = False` and
`Get_Error (Result)` SHALL be one of the `UTF_8_Decode_Error` variants
from FUNC-UTF8-001 corresponding to the first detected violation.

`From_UTF_8` SHALL NOT raise any exception under any input.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
This subsumes both the original raising `From_UTF_8` and the original
`Try_From_UTF_8` from the first draft.  The Result-pattern variant is the
single, total, non-raising ingestion path required by the project's
SPARK-friendly, no-exceptions rule.

The "first detected violation" wording leaves error-detection order
unspecified; backends MAY scan left-to-right and stop on first error,
which is the simplest correct strategy.

Source file: `src/anystrings.ads`
Backend directories: `src/strings/standard/`, `src/strings/wide/`,
`src/strings/wide_wide/`, `src/strings/uxstring/`
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-009
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: From_UTF_8 on an empty string
STATEMENT: >>>
When `Source` is an empty `String` (`""`), `From_UTF_8 (Source)` SHALL
return a Result `R` such that `Is_Ok (R) = True` and `Get_Value (R)` is
the empty `String_Type` value (equivalent to `Null_String`).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-010
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: From_UTF_8 error mapping for canonical invalid sequences
STATEMENT: >>>
`From_UTF_8` SHALL map each canonical class of invalid UTF-8 input to the
corresponding `UTF_8_Decode_Error` variant:

- A bare continuation byte (16#80#-16#BF# at the start of a sequence)
  -> `Invalid_Lead_Byte`.
- A lead byte that announces N continuation bytes, with fewer than N
  remaining bytes -> `Truncated_Sequence`.
- A lead byte followed by a byte outside 16#80#-16#BF#
  -> `Invalid_Continuation`.
- A 2-byte sequence encoding a code point < U+0080, a 3-byte sequence
  encoding a code point < U+0800, or a 4-byte sequence encoding a code
  point < U+10000 -> `Overlong_Encoding`.
- A sequence whose decoded scalar value is in U+D800-U+DFFF
  -> `Surrogate_Code_Point`.
- A sequence whose decoded scalar value is > U+10FFFF
  -> `Code_Point_Out_Of_Range`.

If a single byte sequence matches more than one category, backends MAY
report any matching category, but the chosen mapping SHALL be
deterministic for a given backend.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
This requirement makes the error variants testable.  The "deterministic
for a given backend" clause permits backends that piggy-back on an
external decoder (e.g. UXStrings) to expose whatever ordering that
decoder uses, so long as it is stable.
<<<

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Round-Trip Invariant

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-011
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: From_UTF_8 of To_UTF_8 round-trip identity for surrogate-free values
STATEMENT: >>>
For every `String_Type` value `S` representable by the backend AND
containing no code units in the surrogate range U+D800-U+DFFF, the
following SHALL hold:

    let R := From_UTF_8 (To_UTF_8 (S))
    Is_Ok (R) = True
    Get_Value (R) = S

That is, converting any surrogate-free `String_Type` value to UTF-8 and
back SHALL reproduce the original value without loss, substitution, or
modification.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
The round-trip invariant is restricted to surrogate-free inputs because
FUNC-UTF8-007 mandates surrogate substitution by U+FFFD in `To_UTF_8`,
which is intentionally lossy for surrogate code units.

The inverse round-trip - `To_UTF_8 (Get_Value (From_UTF_8 (B)))` - is
defined only for valid UTF-8 byte strings `B` and is NOT required to
equal `B` byte-for-byte if the backend normalises the representation
(e.g. NFC normalisation); however, if normalisation is applied it MUST
be documented in the backend's package header.

This invariant MUST hold for all four bundled backends and for all
representable surrogate-free `String_Type` values including empty
strings, ASCII-only strings, and strings containing the full
non-surrogate code-point range supported by the backend.
<<<

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Backend Coverage and API Parity

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-012
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: All four bundled backends must implement the UTF-8 formals
STATEMENT: >>>
Each of the four bundled backends SHALL provide concrete implementations
of `To_UTF_8` and `From_UTF_8`:

- `src/strings/standard/`   - Latin-1 / Ada `String` backend
- `src/strings/wide/`       - `Wide_String` / `Wide_Character` backend
- `src/strings/wide_wide/`  - `Wide_Wide_String` / `Wide_Wide_Character`
                              backend
- `src/strings/uxstring/`   - UXStrings backend

Each backend's implementation SHALL be provided in the backend's
`*_operations.ads` and `*_operations.adb` files.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
The UXStrings backend has native UTF-8 support and is expected to have
the simplest implementation.  The `standard` backend transcodes Latin-1
to UTF-8 (a straightforward two-byte expansion for code points 128-255).
The Wide and Wide_Wide backends transcode their respective character
repertoires to/from UTF-8, applying surrogate substitution per
FUNC-UTF8-007.

No implementation algorithm is mandated; only the observable behaviour
specified in FUNC-UTF8-001 through FUNC-UTF8-011 is required.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-UTF8-013
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: UTF-8 formals must not break existing API parity
STATEMENT: >>>
Adding the `To_UTF_8` and `From_UTF_8` formals (and the supporting
`UTF_8_Decode_Error` / `UTF_8_Decode_Result` types) SHALL NOT change the
semantics of any existing formal subprogram, SHALL NOT remove any
existing formal, and SHALL NOT alter the types or parameter modes of any
existing formal.

All four backend instantiation files (`*_strings.ads`) MUST remain
compilable with the updated generic after the formals are added.
<<<

[/SECTION]
